  ' ============================================================
  ' BLACKBOARD NOTE
  ' ============================================================

  note as BlackboardNote
    **Blackboard — Shared problem-solving workspace (engine-owned)**

    The Blackboard is the GSM Sourcer realisation of the canonical
    Blackboard pattern's "shared memory". It is a strictly typed,
    panel-graph-aware data structure on which independent Knowledge
    Sources (KSs) post **Contributions** under the supervision of the
    Sourcer Controller. The Controller is **panel-graph-agnostic in
    semantics** — it knows only the declared DAG and the trigger DSL,
    not what any panel means.

    **Dependency-inversion boundary (DIP)**: the Sourcer Controller
    depends on three engine-owned abstractions only — `Panel`,
    `SourceAnalysisPanel`, `TargetDefinitionPanel`. Concrete source
    analysis panels live in domain Sourcing Packs (e.g. ITIP's
    Repository Sourcing Pack defines `SourceInventory`, `SourceUnit`,
    `SourceRelation`, `SourceInterface`). Concrete target definition
    panels live here in the engine (the GSM spine). The Controller can
    schedule Knowledge Sources, evaluate trigger predicates, and
    reconcile contributions without ever importing a concrete
    `SourceAnalysisPanel` subclass.

    ====

    **Two panel groups**

    1. **Source analysis panels** (Pack-owned). Capture the source-side
       analysis of the artefact under sourcing — files, units,
       relations, interfaces. Each Pack ships its own concrete subclasses
       of `SourceAnalysisPanel`. The engine ships ONLY the abstract base
       class and a placeholder group on this diagram. The Repository
       Sourcing Pack class diagram (in `itip/`) holds the four concrete
       subclasses currently defined.
    2. **Target definition panels** (engine-owned). Carry the GSM spine
       — Definition, Archetype, Statement, Granularity, Evidence,
       Confidence. They are sealed: tenants MAY add Pack-namespaced
       additive contribution types via `pack-manifest.extendsPanels` but
       MAY NOT redefine engine-defined contribution types or add new
       target panels at runtime.

    ====

    **Closed contribution-type sets**

    Every `Panel` declares a closed set of **contribution types** at
    Pack-registration time (one contribution type = one panel property =
    one slot in the JSON Schema sense). A `Contribution` belongs to
    exactly one `(panel, contributionType)` pair, with the contribution
    type drawn from the panel's closed set. Adding a contribution type
    at Run time is rejected (invariant I-3 in the GSM Sourcing
    Blackboard skill).

    ====

    **Conflict resolution and the resolved view**

    Each panel declares a default `ContributionResolver`. KSs may post
    multiple competing Contributions for the same `(panel,
    contributionType, identityKey)` triple; the resolver collapses them
    into a single resolved value visible via
    `BlackboardView.resolved(panel, identityKey)`. Resolvers:
    `highestConfidence`, `firstWriter`, `failOnConflict`,
    `userMediated`, `collectAll`, `compose` (schema-driven per-key
    merge of value objects — used by Statement so multiple
    StatementShapingKSs can populate disjoint archetype fields).
    `ConfidenceSynthesisKS`, `ReconciliationKS`, and ensemble KSs MUST
    use `BlackboardView.contributionsOf(panel, identityKey)` instead —
    `resolved` would already have collapsed the diversity those KSs
    need to reason over.

    ====

    **Engine run state distinct from the Blackboard**

    The Run input (operator intent, scope, focus, profile, resolver
    overrides, external tool bindings, run invariants) does NOT live on
    the Blackboard as a confidence-1.0 hypothesis (a deliberate
    departure from Hearsay-II). It lives in `SourcingRunContext` — a
    **frozen run-scoped engine value, distinct from the Blackboard** —
    accessed via `controlContext.runContext()`. Rationale: the Run
    input is structured Pack-typed metadata, not raw signal that needs
    incremental hypothesis-driven interpretation.

    ====

    **Panel DAG (engine-validated at Pack registration)**

    ```
    SourceInventory  ->  SourceUnit
    SourceUnit       ->  SourceInterface
    SourceUnit       ->  SourceRelation
    {SourceInterface, SourceUnit, SourceRelation} -> Definition  <<bridge>>
    Definition       ->  Archetype
    Archetype        ->  Statement
    Statement        ->  Granularity
    Statement        ->  Evidence
    Evidence         ->  Confidence
    ```

    A KS's `readsPanels` MUST be a subset of the transitive ancestors
    of its `writesPanel` in this DAG. Cross-DAG reads are rejected at
    Pack registration. Cycles are rejected. Note that the four source
    analysis panels appear as concrete subclasses of
    `SourceAnalysisPanel` and live in the Pack diagram; here only the
    abstract class is shown as a placeholder.
  end note

  ' ============================================================
  ' PANEL — abstract root (DIP boundary for the Controller)
  ' ============================================================

  abstract class Panel <<engine>> {
    +getName(): String
    +getOwner(): PanelOwner
    +getContributionTypes(): String[]
    +getDependsOnPanels(): String[]
    +getValueSchemaRef(): URI
    +getDefaultResolver(): ContributionResolver
    +accept(Contribution c): ValidationResult
  }

  note bottom of Panel
    **Panel** — Closed-typed contribution-bearing region of the
    Blackboard.

    **Concept Semantics**:

    - A Panel is a typed region of the Blackboard on which Knowledge
      Sources post Contributions. It is the engine's unit of dependency
      inversion: the Sourcer Controller depends on this abstraction,
      not on any concrete subclass.
    - A Panel declares a CLOSED set of contribution types (formerly
      "slots") at Pack registration time. One contribution type is one
      panel property; one Contribution belongs to exactly one
      `(panel, contributionType)` pair.
    - A Panel is owned either by the **engine** (target definition
      panels — the GSM spine) or by a **Pack** (source analysis panels
      — domain-specific source observations). Owner is sealed at
      registration.
    - The Panel knows its place in the engine-validated panel DAG via
      `getDependsOnPanels()`. The DAG is validated for acyclicity and
      cross-DAG-read containment at Pack registration.

    **Method Semantics**:

    - `getName()`: panel name. SHOULD be Pack-namespaced for source
      analysis panels (e.g. `itip:SourceUnit`); engine-owned target
      panels use unqualified GSM-spine names (Definition, Archetype,
      Statement, Granularity, Evidence, Confidence).
    - `getOwner()`: ENGINE or PACK. Sealed at Pack registration.
    - `getContributionTypes()`: the closed set of contribution-type
      identifiers this panel accepts. Engine-defined contribution types
      on engine-owned panels are sealed. Packs MAY add Pack-namespaced
      additive contribution types via `pack-manifest.extendsPanels`.
    - `getDependsOnPanels()`: panel-DAG edges. Engine-owned panel names
      are allowed for upward bridges from source to target.
    - `getValueSchemaRef()`: versioned `gsmarc://` URI of the panel's
      value JSON Schema. Resolution: lookup as a resource bundled in
      the Pack JAR at `/META-INF/gsmarc/<URI-tail>`. The URI is
      informational + cache key — no network fetch.
    - `getDefaultResolver()`: panel-default `ContributionResolver`.
      RunConfig may override per panel.
    - `accept(Contribution)`: engine-side acceptance check —
      contribution type membership, value-schema validation,
      determinism + cacheKey rule (I-4), kind + checkpointId rule (I-11).

    **Validation Rules**:

    - `getName()` MUST be unique within a Run. The engine collision-
      rejects two Pack manifests declaring the same panel name with
      non-identical `getValueSchemaRef()` or `getContributionTypes()`.
    - `getDependsOnPanels()` MUST NOT introduce a cycle in the DAG.
    - A Contribution accepted by a Panel MUST carry a `slot` that is a
      member of `getContributionTypes()`.
    - A Contribution with `determinism = NON_DETERMINISTIC` MUST carry
      `cacheKey` (I-4).
    - A Contribution with `kind = USER_OVERRIDE` MUST carry
      `checkpointId` (I-11).

    **Derivation Rules**:

    - `getName()` -> `pack-manifest.panels[].name` for PACK panels;
      engine-defined constant for ENGINE panels.
    - `getOwner()` -> ENGINE for built-in engine spine panels; PACK
      otherwise.
    - `getContributionTypes()` -> union of declared `slots` and any
      additive `extendsPanels` contribution types.
    - `getValueSchemaRef()` -> declared `valueSchemaRef`.
    - `getDefaultResolver()` -> per-panel constant declared in the
      engine ontology (target panels) or in the Pack manifest (source
      panels).
  end note

  enum PanelOwner {
    ENGINE
    PACK
  }

  enum ContributionResolver {
    highestConfidence
    firstWriter
    failOnConflict
    userMediated
    collectAll
    compose
  }

  ' ============================================================
  ' SOURCE ANALYSIS PANELS — placeholder group with DIP class only
  ' ============================================================

  package "Source analysis panels (Pack-owned; DIP placeholder)" as SRC {

    abstract class SourceAnalysisPanel <<engine>> {
      +getSourceArtefactKind(): String
      +bridgesToTargetPanel(): String
    }

    note bottom of SourceAnalysisPanel
      **SourceAnalysisPanel** — Abstract Pack-side panel base
      (engine-owned DIP boundary).

      **Concept Semantics**:

      - The abstract base class every Pack-owned source-side panel MUST
        extend. Defines the Sourcer Controller's only dependency on
        Pack code — concrete subclasses are loaded as Pack JARs in
        sandboxed classloaders during the Initializer's Plug-in Loader
        substep, then declared on the Blackboard during the Blackboard
        Builder substep.
      - A source analysis panel captures the **source-side analysis of
        the artefact under sourcing** — what the Pack observes in the
        source material before any GSM-spine identity is asserted.
        Examples in the Repository Sourcing Pack: file inventory, code
        units, code-level relations, exposed interfaces.
      - Its DAG outputs ALWAYS bridge upward into a target definition
        panel (typically `Definition` via `<<bridge>>` edges). Source
        panels never write to the engine spine directly — only KSs do
        (an `IdentificationKS` reads source panels and posts to
        `Definition.Claim`).
      - Concrete subclasses live in the Pack repository, NOT here. See
        `itip/def/repository-sourcing-pack/repository-sourcing-pack.puml`
        for the four concrete classes currently defined.

      **Method Semantics**:

      - `getSourceArtefactKind()`: short identifier of the source-side
        artefact family this panel observes (e.g. `inventory`, `unit`,
        `relation`, `interface`). Free-form; informational only.
      - `bridgesToTargetPanel()`: the name of the target definition
        panel this source panel bridges to via `<<bridge>>` DAG edges.
        Almost always `"Definition"`; reserved for future
        configurations where Packs bridge to a different entry point of
        the target spine.

      **Validation Rules**:

      - `getOwner()` MUST return `PACK` for any concrete
        `SourceAnalysisPanel`.
      - `bridgesToTargetPanel()` MUST resolve to a registered engine-
        owned `TargetDefinitionPanel` name.
      - The panel's `getName()` SHOULD be Pack-namespaced.

      **Derivation Rules**:

      - The Sourcer Controller schedules Knowledge Sources by trigger
        predicates over `Panel` (the abstract). It never type-tests for
        a concrete `SourceAnalysisPanel` subclass.
      - Concrete subclasses are introduced by the Pack's
        `pack-manifest.panels[]` declarations and instantiated by the
        Blackboard Builder.
    end note

    note as SrcPlaceholder
      **Concrete subclasses live in the Pack diagrams.**
      For the Repository Sourcing Pack, see:
      `itip/def/repository-sourcing-pack/repository-sourcing-pack.puml`
      (currently: `SourceInventory`, `SourceUnit`,
      `SourceRelation`, `SourceInterface`).
    end note
  }

  ' ============================================================
  ' TARGET DEFINITION PANELS — engine-owned GSM spine
  ' ============================================================

  package "Target definition panels (engine-owned; GSM spine)" as TGT {

    abstract class TargetDefinitionPanel <<engine>> {
      +getTargetIdentityKeyOf(Contribution c): String
      +getSubjectKindRange(): DefinitionSubjectKind[]
    }

    note bottom of TargetDefinitionPanel
      **TargetDefinitionPanel** — Abstract engine-side panel base
      (GSM spine).

      **Concept Semantics**:

      - The abstract base class for the six engine-owned panels that
        materialise the GSM spine on the Blackboard: Definition,
        Archetype, Statement, Granularity, Evidence, Confidence.
      - All target definition panels are KEYED BY `identityKey` — the
        identifier of the GSM subject under definition. The
        Definition panel MINTS the identity key (`Claim`) and may
        merge prior keys (`Merge`); all other target panels CARRY
        `targetIdentityKey` on every Contribution to anchor it to a
        Definition.
      - Tenants MAY add Pack-namespaced additive contribution types
        via `pack-manifest.extendsPanels` (e.g. `itip:Refined` on
        `Statement`). Engine-defined contribution types are sealed.
      - The Pipeline (`ValidatedAscriptionDraft`,
        `RegisteredAscription`) consumes the converged target tuple per
        identity. Pipeline records are NOT panels (I-8).

      **Method Semantics**:

      - `getTargetIdentityKeyOf(Contribution)`: extracts the
        identity-key the Contribution targets. For `Definition` this is
        `value.identityKey`; for the other five panels it is
        `value.targetIdentityKey`.
      - `getSubjectKindRange()`: the set of GSM subject kinds this
        panel may carry data for. For `Definition` this is the full
        eight-primitive enum; downstream panels narrow only when their
        per-subject semantics require it (most accept all eight).

      **Validation Rules**:

      - `getOwner()` MUST return `ENGINE`.
      - For non-Definition panels, every accepted Contribution MUST
        carry a non-null `targetIdentityKey` resolving to a Definition
        present on the Blackboard.
      - For `Definition.Merge` Contributions, every entry of
        `supersedesIdentityKeys` MUST resolve to a prior
        `Definition.Claim` Contribution on the Blackboard.

      **Derivation Rules**:

      - Engine-owned; instantiated by the Blackboard Builder substep
        from a fixed engine ontology (the six concrete subclasses
        below).
    end note

    enum DefinitionSubjectKind {
      STRUCTURE
      MECHANISM
      EFFECTOR
      RECEPTOR
      INTERACTION
      ARCHETYPE
      DIRECTIVE
      NORM
    }

    class Definition <<engine,target>> {
      +getId(): String
      +getName(): String
      +getOwner(): PanelOwner
      +getContributionTypes(): String[]
      +getDefaultResolver(): ContributionResolver
      +getSubjectKind(Contribution c): DefinitionSubjectKind
      +getIdentityKey(Contribution c): String
    }

    note bottom of Definition
      **Definition** — Identity-binding panel of the GSM spine.

      **Concept Semantics**:

      - The Definition panel decides WHICH source items collectively
        define ONE GSM subject (Structure, Mechanism, Effector,
        Receptor, Interaction, Archetype, Directive, Norm). It is the
        join key for every other target panel.
      - Two contribution types (closed):
        - `Claim` — an `IdentificationKS` asserts an identity for a
          subject based on source-panel evidence.
        - `Merge` — a `ReconciliationKS` asserts that two prior
          identities describe the same subject and supersedes them.
      - Default resolver: `highestConfidence`. Conflicts with two
        equally-confident `Claim`s on the same surface key are
        escalated to a Checkpoint (`failOnConflict` / `userMediated`).

      **Method Semantics**:

      - `getSubjectKind(Contribution)`: returns the GSM primitive kind
        the Definition asserts (sealed eight-value enum).
      - `getIdentityKey(Contribution)`: stable identifier of the
        subject; used as the join key by all downstream target panels.

      **Validation Rules**:

      - `Claim` Contributions MUST carry `subjectKind`, `identityKey`,
        and `supportingSourceIds` (Contribution ids from source
        analysis panels).
      - `Merge` Contributions MUST carry `supersedesIdentityKeys`
        (non-empty) and a `targetIdentityKey` for the surviving
        identity.

      **Merge precedence rule (Definition.Merge vs Granularity.mergeWith)**:

      - `Definition.Merge` is identity-time merging (pre-Statement),
        based on source-side signals only.
      - `Granularity.mergeWith` is post-statement consolidation,
        informed by the populated Statement.
      - When both apply to the same identity, `Granularity.mergeWith`
        wins (more informed). The Override Cascader supersedes the
        earlier `Definition.Merge` and re-fires downstream triggers
        per I-11 cascade scope.

      **Derivation Rules**:

      - `getOwner()` -> `ENGINE` (constant).
      - `getName()` -> `"Definition"` (constant).
      - `getContributionTypes()` -> `{"Claim", "Merge"}` plus any
        Pack-namespaced extensions.
      - `getDefaultResolver()` -> `highestConfidence`.
    end note

    class Archetype <<engine,target>> {
      +getId(): String
      +getName(): String
      +getOwner(): PanelOwner
      +getContributionTypes(): String[]
      +getDefaultResolver(): ContributionResolver
      +getArchetypeRef(Contribution c): URI
    }

    note bottom of Archetype
      **Archetype** — Archetype-application panel of the GSM spine.

      **Concept Semantics**:

      - Captures which GSM Archetype(s) apply to a given Definition.
        One contribution type (closed):
        - `Application` — an `ArchetypeKS` posts one
          archetype reference per Contribution. Multiple
          Contributions per identity are union-resolved.
      - Default resolver: `collectAll` (union over Applications). The
        Pipeline reads the union as the resolved archetype set for the
        Definition.

      **Method Semantics**:

      - `getArchetypeRef(Contribution)`: versioned `gsmarc://` URI of
        the GSM Archetype Definition.

      **Validation Rules**:

      - Every `Application` Contribution MUST carry a non-null
        `targetIdentityKey` and a non-null `archetypeRef` resolving to
        a registered Archetype.
      - The asserted Archetype's structural-base (allOf chain) MUST
        be compatible with the `Definition.subjectKind` (e.g. a
        `Mechanism`-rooted Archetype cannot be applied to a Definition
        whose `subjectKind` is `STRUCTURE`).

      **Derivation Rules**:

      - `getOwner()` -> `ENGINE`.
      - `getName()` -> `"Archetype"`.
      - `getContributionTypes()` -> `{"Application"}` plus extensions.
      - `getDefaultResolver()` -> `collectAll`.
    end note

    class Statement <<engine,target>> {
      +getId(): String
      +getName(): String
      +getOwner(): PanelOwner
      +getContributionTypes(): String[]
      +getDefaultResolver(): ContributionResolver
      +getStatement(Contribution c): JsonObject
      +getCompleteness(Contribution c): Completeness
      +getShapingMethod(Contribution c): String
    }

    note bottom of Statement
      **Statement** — Statement-shaping panel of the GSM spine.

      **Concept Semantics**:

      - Holds populated GSM statement payloads conforming to the
        resolved Archetype's JSON Schema. One contribution type
        (closed):
        - `Draft` — a `StatementShapingKS` posts a partial or full
          statement payload.
      - Default resolver: `compose`. Multiple StatementShapingKSs may
        post Contributions populating disjoint archetype fields; the
        engine's schema-driven per-key merge assembles the union into
        the resolved Statement. This is the panel-level realisation of
        cooperative shaping.

      **Method Semantics**:

      - `getStatement(Contribution)`: the (possibly partial)
        archetype-typed JSON statement.
      - `getCompleteness(Contribution)`: `partial` or `full`. The
        Pipeline's Validation stage rejects compositions that remain
        `partial` after resolution.
      - `getShapingMethod(Contribution)`: free-form identifier of the
        shaping technique used (`structural-extraction`,
        `llm-completion`, ...). Informational; used by Evidence and
        Confidence KSs.

      **Validation Rules**:

      - Every `Draft` Contribution MUST carry a non-null
        `targetIdentityKey` resolving to an Archetype-resolved
        Definition.
      - `getStatement()` MUST validate against the resolved Archetype
        schema for the targeted Definition (the engine performs an
        incremental schema check at post time).
      - LLM-driven Contributions MUST carry `determinism =
        NON_DETERMINISTIC` and a `cacheKey` (I-4).

      **Derivation Rules**:

      - `getOwner()` -> `ENGINE`.
      - `getName()` -> `"Statement"`.
      - `getContributionTypes()` -> `{"Draft"}` plus extensions.
      - `getDefaultResolver()` -> `compose`.
    end note

    class Granularity <<engine,target>> {
      +getId(): String
      +getName(): String
      +getOwner(): PanelOwner
      +getContributionTypes(): String[]
      +getDefaultResolver(): ContributionResolver
      +getVerdict(Contribution c): GranularityVerdict
      +getMergeWithIdentityKey(Contribution c): String
      +getSplitIntoIdentityKeys(Contribution c): String[]
    }

    enum GranularityVerdict {
      keep
      drop
      splitInto
      mergeWith
    }

    note bottom of Granularity
      **Granularity** — Post-statement consolidation panel of the GSM
      spine.

      **Concept Semantics**:

      - Holds per-Definition consolidation verdicts AFTER Statement
        shaping. One contribution type (closed):
        - `Verdict` — a `GranularityKS` posts one of four verdicts:
          `keep`, `drop`, `splitInto`, `mergeWith`.
      - Distinct from `Definition.Merge`: Granularity runs AFTER
        Statement shaping, so its `mergeWith` verdict is informed by
        the populated GSM statement and TAKES PRECEDENCE over any
        earlier identity-time `Definition.Merge` for the same identity
        (Override Cascader supersedes the earlier `Merge`).
      - Default resolver: `highestConfidence`.

      **Method Semantics**:

      - `getVerdict(Contribution)`: one of the four enum values.
      - `getMergeWithIdentityKey(Contribution)`: required when
        `verdict = mergeWith` — the surviving identity.
      - `getSplitIntoIdentityKeys(Contribution)`: required when
        `verdict = splitInto` — the new identities introduced.

      **Validation Rules**:

      - Every `Verdict` Contribution MUST carry a non-null
        `targetIdentityKey`.
      - `mergeWith` Contributions MUST carry a non-null
        `mergeWithIdentityKey` resolving to a Definition on the
        Blackboard.
      - `splitInto` Contributions MUST carry a non-empty
        `splitIntoIdentityKeys[]`. Each new identity yields a fresh
        Definition + downstream-target re-fire.

      **Derivation Rules**:

      - `getOwner()` -> `ENGINE`.
      - `getName()` -> `"Granularity"`.
      - `getContributionTypes()` -> `{"Verdict"}` plus extensions.
      - `getDefaultResolver()` -> `highestConfidence`.
    end note

    class Evidence <<engine,target>> {
      +getId(): String
      +getName(): String
      +getOwner(): PanelOwner
      +getContributionTypes(): String[]
      +getDefaultResolver(): ContributionResolver
      +getWeight(Contribution c): double
      +getEvidenceKind(Contribution c): String
    }

    note bottom of Evidence
      **Evidence** — Confidence-feeding panel of the GSM spine.

      **Concept Semantics**:

      - Aggregates positive or negative signals about a Definition's
        Statement and surrounding context. One contribution type
        (closed):
        - `Signal` — an `EvidenceCollectionKS` posts one weighted
          signal per Contribution. Additive — every Signal counts.
      - Default resolver: `collectAll`. The resolved view is the full
        list of Signals; `ConfidenceSynthesisKS` reads the list (via
        `contributionsOf`, NOT `resolved`) to compute a Confidence
        Score.

      **Method Semantics**:

      - `getWeight(Contribution)`: signal weight in `[-1, 1]`. Negative
        weights count against the Statement.
      - `getEvidenceKind(Contribution)`: free-form classifier
        (`test-pass`, `test-fail`, `interface-coherence`,
        `relation-density`, ...). Informational.

      **Validation Rules**:

      - Every `Signal` Contribution MUST carry a non-null
        `targetIdentityKey` and a `weight` in `[-1, 1]`.

      **Derivation Rules**:

      - `getOwner()` -> `ENGINE`.
      - `getName()` -> `"Evidence"`.
      - `getContributionTypes()` -> `{"Signal"}` plus extensions.
      - `getDefaultResolver()` -> `collectAll`.
    end note

    class Confidence <<engine,target>> {
      +getId(): String
      +getName(): String
      +getOwner(): PanelOwner
      +getContributionTypes(): String[]
      +getDefaultResolver(): ContributionResolver
      +getScore(Contribution c): double
      +getTargetPanel(Contribution c): String
    }

    note bottom of Confidence
      **Confidence** — Confidence-synthesis panel of the GSM spine.

      **Concept Semantics**:

      - Holds the synthesised confidence score per Definition (and
        optionally per downstream target panel: Definition, Archetype,
        Statement, Granularity). One contribution type (closed):
        - `Score` — a `ConfidenceSynthesisKS` posts one Score
          Contribution per identity (and optional `targetPanel`).
      - Default resolver: `highestConfidence`. The Pipeline's
        Validation stage MAY require a minimum Score per identity
        before producing a `ValidatedAscriptionDraft`.
      - The trigger DSL exposes the predicate
        `confidenceBelow(identity, threshold)` for revision predicates
        — a low Score can re-fire upstream KSs even when their nominal
        upstream contribution is already resolved.

      **Method Semantics**:

      - `getScore(Contribution)`: synthesised score in `[0, 1]`.
      - `getTargetPanel(Contribution)`: name of the target panel the
        Score qualifies. One of `{Definition, Archetype, Statement,
        Granularity}`.

      **Validation Rules**:

      - Every `Score` Contribution MUST carry a non-null
        `targetIdentityKey` and a `score` in `[0, 1]`.
      - `targetPanel` MUST resolve to a registered engine-owned
        `TargetDefinitionPanel` other than `Evidence` and other than
        `Confidence` itself.

      **Derivation Rules**:

      - `getOwner()` -> `ENGINE`.
      - `getName()` -> `"Confidence"`.
      - `getContributionTypes()` -> `{"Score"}` plus extensions.
      - `getDefaultResolver()` -> `highestConfidence`.
    end note
  }

  ' ============================================================
  ' CONTRIBUTION (anchor type)
  ' ============================================================

  class Contribution <<engine>> {
    +getId(): String
    +getPanel(): String
    +getSlot(): String
    +getValue(): JsonObject
    +getTargetIdentityKey(): String
    +getContributorKsId(): URI
    +getKind(): ContributionKind
    +getCheckpointId(): String
    +getDeterminism(): Determinism
    +getCacheKey(): String
    +getDerivedFrom(): String[]
    +getSupersedes(): String[]
    +getCreatedAt(): Instant
  }

  enum ContributionKind {
    KS_DERIVED
    INSTRUCTIONS_DERIVED
    USER_OVERRIDE
  }

  enum Determinism {
    DETERMINISTIC
    NON_DETERMINISTIC
  }

  note bottom of Contribution
    **Contribution** — Atomic post on the Blackboard.

    **Concept Semantics**:

    - The unit of work on the Blackboard. A Contribution is posted by
      a Knowledge Source (or by the Initializer for
      `INSTRUCTIONS_DERIVED`, or by a Reviewer at a Checkpoint for
      `USER_OVERRIDE`).
    - Belongs to exactly ONE `(panel, slot)` pair, where `slot` is a
      member of the panel's `getContributionTypes()`.
    - The engine attaches `id`, `contributorKsId`, `kind`,
      `derivedFrom`, `createdAt`, `supersedes`. KSs cannot fabricate
      them.
    - User overrides cascade via the Override Cascader: cascade scope
      is the closure over `derivedFrom` UNION all downstream panels for
      the same `targetIdentityKey` (I-11).

    **Method Semantics**:

    - `getKind()`: provenance class — `KS_DERIVED` (default),
      `INSTRUCTIONS_DERIVED` (Initializer-posted before Control
      starts), `USER_OVERRIDE` (Reviewer-posted at a Checkpoint).
    - `getCheckpointId()`: required when `kind = USER_OVERRIDE`;
      identifies the suspended Checkpoint.
    - `getDeterminism()`: `DETERMINISTIC` (structural extraction) or
      `NON_DETERMINISTIC` (LLM-driven).
    - `getCacheKey()`: required when `determinism = NON_DETERMINISTIC`
      (I-4). Stable hash of `(KS id, KS version, all read inputs)`.
    - `getDerivedFrom()`: ids of Contributions read by the KS to
      produce this one. Used by the Override Cascader.
    - `getSupersedes()`: ids this Contribution supersedes (engine-set).

    **Validation Rules**:

    - `(panel, slot)` MUST be a registered pair: `slot` is a member of
      `panel.getContributionTypes()`.
    - `targetIdentityKey` is required for non-source panels (target
      definition panels carry it; source analysis panels MAY omit it).
    - `determinism = NON_DETERMINISTIC` -> `cacheKey` MUST be present
      (I-4).
    - `kind = USER_OVERRIDE` -> `checkpointId` MUST be present (I-11).

    **Derivation Rules**:

    - All engine-attached fields are derived at post time from the
      executing KS's identity, the Contribution's read set, and the
      current Run state.
  end note

  ' ============================================================
  ' SOURCING RUN CONTEXT (engine run state)
  ' ============================================================

  package "Engine run state\n(distinct from the Blackboard)" as ERS {

    class SourcingRunContext <<engine,immutable>> {
      +getRunId(): String
      +getProfile(): JsonObject
      +getInstructionsPayload(): JsonObject
      +getScope(): Scope
      +getFocus(): Focus
      +getRunInvariants(): RunInvariants
      +getResolverOverrides(): Map<String, ContributionResolver>
      +getExternalToolBindings(): ExternalToolBinding[]
    }

    note bottom of SourcingRunContext
      **SourcingRunContext** — Frozen run-scoped engine value (NOT a
      panel).

      **Concept Semantics**:

      - Built by the `Initializer` in five substeps: Plug-in Loader ->
        Instructions Provider -> Scope Builder -> Focus Builder ->
        freeze `SourcingRunContext` -> Blackboard Builder. The freeze
        happens BEFORE the Blackboard Builder stands up the panels, so
        the Run state is fully determined before any KS can run.
      - Read by all KSs as a read-only value via
        `controlContext.runContext()`. The deliberate departure from
        Hearsay-II: the Run input lives HERE, not as a Blackboard
        hypothesis (I-1).

      **Method Semantics**:

      - `getProfile()`: operator-supplied profile (free-form Pack-typed).
      - `getInstructionsPayload()`: forwarded verbatim from RunConfig
        to the Pack's Instructions Provider.
      - `getScope()`: derived `{ inclusion[], exclusion[],
        axisDecision }`.
      - `getFocus()`: derived `{ priorityBands[] }`.
      - `getRunInvariants()`: `{ maxIterations, quiescenceThreshold,
        determinismMode, completionPredicate? }`.
      - `getResolverOverrides()`: per-panel resolver overrides.
      - `getExternalToolBindings()`: operator-narrowed bindings of
        the Pack's `requestedExternalTools[]` to host paths.

      **Validation Rules**:

      - Frozen after Initialization. Any mutation attempt MUST raise.
      - `runInvariants.completionPredicate`, when set, MUST parse as a
        valid trigger-DSL expression.

      **Derivation Rules**:

      - Built once per Run by the Initializer's substeps.
    end note
  }

